  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 936 PM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nSkies across the region are mostly clear with some thin high\nclouds working eastward. Some thicker cloud cover has pushed \ninto northeast UT ahead of the trough moving ashore on the West\nCoast tonight. Cloud cover will be notable early Monday \nmorning, as it could hinder some of the fog and low stratus we \nsaw develop on Sunday morning. Satellite imagery at this hour \nshows a little bit of stratus developing in southwest CO. The \nabundant surface moisture down there will encourage fog, should \nwe radiate out properly overnight. Dewpoint depressions around \nDurango, Cortez, and Pagosa Springs are already near zero.\n\nAnother warm and mild day will start the work week, with\ntemperatures around 5 degrees above average. Light winds and \nsunshine stick around Monday. Thin high clouds begin to invade \nthe region ahead of Tuesday's storm. This will usher in a \nseries of pattern shifts across the region. Strong southwesterly\nwinds develop Tuesday on the backside of the departing high \npressure. This will push temperatures up nearly 10 degrees above\nnormal and bring moisture back into the region along a weak \nfrontal boundary. These warm temperatures will keep snow levels \nin the 9-10k range for our mountain groups, with rain in the \nvalleys below Tuesday afternoon and early Wednesday morning. \nSnow totals have ebbed some on this latest model run. Winter \nheadlines could come into play across the San Juans on Tuesday \nnight. Gusty winds beneath a potent jet streak could stir up a \nlot of blowing snow on Tuesday evening. Travelers will want to \nstay abreast of local conditions on mountain roads, as \nvisibility and road conditions could deteriorate rather quickly \nin the evening hours as the frontal boundary works across the \nregion. \n\nSnow will taper pretty quickly around daybreak Wednesday, as\nthis system crosses the Divide. A few lingering snow showers\nwill continue along the Divide Wednesday morning. Commuters on\nthe passes are likely to see some slick roads too. Cooler and\ndrier conditions develop on Wednesday with temperatures 10 to 15\ndegrees colder behind the front. A weak transitory ridge moves\noverhead Wednesday and Thursday as another more organized \nsystem comes ashore out west.\n\nLATE WEEK SYSTEM: \n\nForecast models continue to show another system arriving late\nThursday night through Saturday morning. Models still show an \nopen wave setup. Precipitation totals continue to vary by model \nrun, with the central and northern mountains of Colorado picking\nup additional snow in the latest runs. Differences among the \nmodels with regards to strength and timing keep confidence low \nattm. Most of the moisture is expected to arrive ahead of the \ncold front, making timing critical. Snow levels could drop to \nsome valley floors Friday night into Saturday morning. For now, \nhowever, the specifics remain uncertain.",
